BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602q1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T082226Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T152500
SUMMARY:Power Sequencing for Enumberable Busses - Driver Core Integrations?
LOCATION:Club B+C\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Driver Core MC · Chen-Yu Tsai · https://lpc.events/event/20/c
 ontributions/2502/\n\nOn x86 / ACPI platforms\, devices on enumerable buss
 es can normally be seen directly by the OS. On device tree platforms\, the
 se devices sometimes require extra power sequencing like toggling regulato
 r supplies or GPIO lines. Over the years most of these cases have been sol
 ved\, but there are still some gaps.\n\nAs of kernel version v7.0\, suppor
 t for power sequencing generic PCI devices\, ones that have no extra toggl
 es that are not part of PCI specification\, and M.2 M-key slots is availab
 le [[link][1]]. v7.1 then adds support for the PCI part of E-key slots [[l
 ink][2]].\n\nSupport for the USB part of M.2 E-key slots is WIP by the aut
 hor [[link][3]]. Support for onboard USB devices and USB type A connectors
  is provided\nby the onboard device driver.\n\nThis session intends to giv
 e a quick overview of the current status and discuss whether parts of this
  could be moved or integrated at the driver core level. These include:\n\n
  - Bus code integration for acquiring power sequencers\n - Creating stub p
 latform devices to provide power control functionality\n   - USB onboard d
 evices\n   - PCI pwrctrl devices\n\nSuch mechanisms could then be reused f
 or the MDIO bus.\n\n  [1]: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-pci/20260107-pci-
 m2-v5-0-8173d8a72641@oss.qualcomm.com/\n  [2]: https://lore.kernel.org/all
 /20260326-pci-m2-e-v7-0-43324a7866e6@oss.qualcomm.com/\n  [3]: https://lor
 e.kernel.org/all/20260916075745.3549953-1-wenst@chromium.org/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2502/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602r1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T082226Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T152500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T155000
SUMMARY:Reviving early platform drivers
LOCATION:Club B+C\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Driver Core MC · Bartosz Golaszewski · https://lpc.events/eve
 nt/20/contributions/2503/\n\nCertain critical subsystems - clocks\, timers
  and interrupt controllers - sometimes need to be initialized before drive
 r core is made available in driver_init(). To that end\, we provide a set 
 of macros: IRQCHIP_DECLARE()\, CLK_OF_DECLARE()\, TIMER_OF_DECLARE() which
  allow the kernel to call initialization functions based on compatibles ei
 ther before reaching the point where actual platform devices matching thes
 e compatibles can be created or *instead* of creating them essentially byp
 assing the driver model entirely.\n\nThis results in the initialization ro
 utines not being able to use many APIs only available to real drivers and 
 - for many modules - never registering with the driver core.\n\nI've worke
 d on the idea of unifying these paths with a concept of "early platform dr
 ivers" back in 2018. That work never got anywhere but the "hacky" approach
  for early setup remains.\n\nI'd like to re-discuss the idea\, it's pros a
 nd cons and provide possible solutions for the main contention point raise
 d last time: the fact that my series did nothing to automatically convert 
 existing invocations of the _DECLARE() macros to using early platform driv
 ers.\n\n[1] https://lore.kernel.org/all/20180511162028.20616-1-brgl@bgdev.
 pl/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2503/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602s1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T082226Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T155000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T161500
SUMMARY:Evolving support for sync_state to subsystems beyond genpd
LOCATION:Club B+C\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Driver Core MC · Ulf Hansson · https://lpc.events/event/20/co
 ntributions/2504/\n\nAt last LPC in Tokyo we discussed about the limitatio
 ns of the sync_state support that quite recently was added to the generic 
 PM domain (genpd) subsystem. The conclusion was to mainly focus on making 
 it more fine grained\, as this should address most of the problems. Attemp
 ts to implement this has been submitted to LKML [1]. Discussion and iterat
 ions of the series are moving forward\, but a solution is yet to be landed
 .\n\nIn this regards\, we also have the need to extend the support for syn
 c_state to more subsystems beyond genpd\, to move away from the broken the
  "disable unused" features that each subsystem currently provides. Moreove
 r\, ideally we prefer the support for sync_state to be adopted on a subsys
 tem basis\, rather than relying on a per driver based implementation\, whi
 ch isn't scaling. Attempts have been made to add support to the regulator 
 and clock subsystems\, while the support in the interconnect subsystem nee
 ds improvements.\n\nLet's discuss these topics and in particular how we ca
 n make subsystem specific implementations to coexist and play along with e
 ach other.\n\n[1]\n[PATCH v3 00/13] driver core / pmdomain: Add support fo
 r fined grained sync_state\nhttps://lore.kernel.org/all/20260508123910.114
 273-1-ulf.hansson@linaro.org/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2504/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602t1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T082226Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T161500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T164000
SUMMARY:Constification of sysfs attribute structures
LOCATION:Club B+C\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Driver Core MC · Thomas Weißschuh · https://lpc.events/event
 /20/contributions/2505/\n\nSysfs attributes are used throughout the kernel
  to implement UAPI.\nSubsystems either use common attributes\, like `kobj_
 attribute` and `device_attr` or define their own wrapper structures.\nThes
 e structures are only descriptors defining the behavior of an attribute an
 d normally never change.\n\nHistorically the attribute however are not mar
 ked as `const` and could be modified through their various callbacks. Such
  modifications are inherently racy and therefore bug-prone or even a vecto
 r for attackers to redirect control-flow.\n\nFor some time I have been wor
 king on making it possible to mark all the different attribute structures 
 as `const` to fix these issues.\n\nAgenda:\n* Problem statement (see above
 )\n* Current state\n  * Which attributes *can* be marked `const` today?\n 
  * Which attributes are already converted.\n* Discussion (see below)\n\n* 
 Discussion\n\nDiscussion topics:\n* Which attribute types are still missin
 g?\n* How can subsystem maintainers convert their custom attribute types?\
 n* How to actually convert all the structure instances throughout the tree
 ?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2505/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602u1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T082226Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T171000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T173500
SUMMARY:Hardware Cross-Dependencies - Solving the Unsolvable
LOCATION:Club B+C\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Driver Core MC · AngeloGioacchino Del Regno\, Sebastian Reiche
 l · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2501/\n\nWe are seeing hard
  to solve cross-dependencies between different SoC subsystems (Rockchip\, 
 MediaTek\, etc.). For example a power domain needing an I2C regulator\, bu
 t the I2C regulator needing the I2C bus and the I2C bus driver needing a (
 different) power domain. This creates a cyclic dependency\, since the powe
 r domains (or clocks) are usually all behind a single device.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2501/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602v1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T082226Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T173500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T180000
SUMMARY:Synx: Cross-core synchronization
LOCATION:Club B+C\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Driver Core MC · Pravin Kumar Ravi · https://lpc.events/event
 /20/contributions/2507/\n\n## Abstract\n\nModern SoCs increasingly run par
 ts of a single pipeline (AI/ML\,\nvision\, camera\, graphics\, sensors) ac
 ross a mix of Linux drivers\nand firmwares on remote processors (NPUs and 
 AI processors\, ISPs\,\ncompanion cores). Coordinating that pipeline requi
 res\nsynchronization objects that can be created\, synchronously or\nasync
 hronously waited on\, signaled\, and released by *any*\nparticipant core\,
  Linux and non-Linux\, with lifetime tracked\nsomewhere no single core own
 s outright.\n\n`dma_fence` solves the local version of this problem well\,
  but its\nlifetime and callback model assumes a single kernel's view of th
 e\nworld. We've developed Synx\, a global handle/refcount table for\nthis 
 exact cross-processor case\, and posted an RFC to dri-devel\nand linux-arm
 -msm describing the model and the specific properties\ndma-fence doesn't c
 urrently cover (refer supporting links).\n\nLetting any two remote process
 ors signal each other directly\,\nwithout routing through the Linux host\,
  cuts latency and avoids an\nunnecessary CPU wake-up. The solution has sho
 wn power and\nperformance benefits in the last few generations of Qualcomm
 \nmobile and XR chipsets\, and is gathering more use cases\,\nspecifically
  ones involving AI pipelines.\n\nChristian König redirects us to solve re
 mote signaling in\nuserspace: a userspace fence/signaling point model base
 d on\ndma-buf\, citing XE's userspace wait support\, `eventfd`\, and ROCm\
 nevents as precedent\, with possible common ground centered around\n`event
 fd`.\n\n## Discussion points\n\n(Supposed to evolve as email thread develo
 ps)\n- the viability of a userspace vs. kernel-space fence model\n- the cu
 rrent remote-signaling solutions from other vendors\n- scope as standalone
  or extend current framework. Define\n  interfaces.\n- model the subsystem
  crash recovery and cleanup\n\n## Key people\n\n- Bartosz Golaszewski\n- D
 mitry Baryshkov\, Srinivas Kandagatla (Driver Core MC\; also\n  Qualcomm c
 olleagues who reviewed Synx internally)\n- Christian König (engaged on th
 e RFC thread)\n- Faith Ekstrand / Xe authors (cited by König)\n\n## Suppo
 rting links\n- https://lore.kernel.org/dri-devel/5f90bb35-994e-48bd-bf49-3
 001dcefa2ee@amd.com/\n- https://lore.kernel.org/linux-arm-msm/202608060519
 15.2234481-1-pravinku@quicinc.com/\n- formatted duplicate @ https://lore.k
 ernel.org/dri-devel/20260806051915.2234481-1-pravinku@quicinc.com/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2507/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602w1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T082226Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T180000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T182500
SUMMARY:Managing IRQ mapping and deferred probing for ACPI static tables de
 vices
LOCATION:Club B+C\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Driver Core MC · Lorenzo Pieralisi · https://lpc.events/event
 /20/contributions/2506/\n\nIn ACPI based system\,devices can be created ou
 t of ACPI static tables entries (eg  ARM64 IORT\, GTDT). For those devices
 \, the GSI HW interrupt number is retrieved by reading table specific fiel
 ds that are different for different static tables. Devices created out of 
 static ACPI tables might be created before the interrupt controller driver
 s their GSI interrupt is routed to is probed\, which means that when the p
 latform device is created the IRQ domain that should be used to map the GS
 I into a virtual IRQ may not be registered yet.\n\nThis leaves us with two
  issues:\n\n - device drivers for devices created out of static tables can
  probe only after the interrupt controller driver their IRQ is routed to h
 as probed\n - In order to map the virtual IRQ for those devices at device 
 driver probe time\, the static table GSI HW IRQ number must be stashed som
 ewhere in the device object so that it can be retrieved and mapped to a vi
 rtual IRQ when the device driver is actually probed\n\nPrototyping for thi
 s solution is under way and a solution for ACPI namespace devices was alre
 ady posted[1] (but that can't solve the problem for devices that are creat
 ed out of static table entries).\n\nThis session would help define a way f
 orward.\n\n[1] https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20260505-gic-v5-acpi-iwb-probe
 -deferral-v1-0-b37b85998362@kernel.org/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2506/
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
